<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boven-Digoel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1927</span> · Also known as Boven Digul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boven-Digoel was an internment camp on the upper Digul River in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial government in 1927 after the failed communist uprisings of 1926–1927. Isolated by jungle and swamp and rife with malaria, it held more than a thousand political prisoners and their families, among them the nationalists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Mohammad Hatta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir">Sutan Sjahrir</a> in 1935–1936. It became a symbol of colonial repression in Indonesian memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boven-digoel.jpg" alt="Boven-Digoel" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Envapid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lokasi_Papua_Selatan_Kabupaten_Boven_Digoel.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
